Article 1 Cases directly accepted by a People's Court as private prosecutions include:
(1) Cases to be handled only upon complaint:
1. Cases of insult or defamation (as provided for in Article 246 of the Criminal Law, except those that seriously endanger social order and national interests);
2. Cases of interference with the freedom of marriage by violence (as provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 257 of the Criminal Law);
3. Cases of maltreatment (as provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 260 of the Criminal Law);
4. Cases of embezzlement (as provided for in Article 270 of the Criminal Law).
(2) Minor criminal cases for which the People's Procuratorate has not initiated a public prosecution and the victim has evidence to prove:
1. Cases of intentional injury (as provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 234 of the Criminal Law);
2. Cases of illegal entry into a residence (as provided for in Article 245 of the Criminal Law);
3. Cases of infringement upon the right to freedom of communication (as provided for in Article 252 of the Criminal Law);
4. Cases of bigamy (as provided for in Article 258 of the Criminal Law);
5. Cases of abandonment (as provided for in Article 261 of the Criminal Law);
6. Cases of producing and selling fake or substandard commodities (as provided for in Section 1 of Chapter III of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law, except those that seriously endanger social order and national interests);
7. Cases of infringement upon intellectual property rights (as provided for in Section 7 of Chapter III of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law, except those that seriously endanger social order and national interests);
8. Cases provided for in Chapter IV and Chapter V of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law for which the defendant may be sentenced to a punishment of not more than three years of fixed-term imprisonment.
For cases specified in this item, if the victim directly files a prosecution with the People's Court, the People's Court shall accept it according to law. For those with insufficient evidence that may be accepted by a public security organ, or for which it is believed that the defendant may be sentenced to a punishment of not less than three years of fixed-term imprisonment, the victim shall be informed to report the case to the public security organ, or the case shall be transferred to the public security organ for filing and investigation.
(3) Cases where the victim has evidence to prove that the defendant's act of infringing upon the victim's personal or property rights shall be subject to criminal liability according to law, and has evidence to prove that a complaint has been filed, but the public security organ or the People's Procuratorate has not investigated the defendant's criminal liability.

Article 15 A Primary People's Court shall transfer to an Intermediate People's Court for trial a criminal case of first instance that may be punishable by life imprisonment or the death penalty.
A Primary People's Court may request to transfer to an Intermediate People's Court for trial any of the following criminal cases of first instance:
(1) Major or complex cases;
(2) New types of difficult cases;
(3) Cases of general guiding significance in the application of law.
If a case needs to be transferred to an Intermediate People's Court for trial, a written request for transfer shall be submitted to the Intermediate People's Court at the latest fifteen days before the expiration of the trial period, after reporting to and obtaining a decision from the president of the court. The Intermediate People's Court shall make a decision within ten days after receiving the request. If the transfer is not approved, a decision disapproving the transfer shall be issued, and the requesting court shall try the case according to law; if the transfer is approved, a decision approving the transfer shall be issued, and the People's Procuratorate at the same level shall be notified in writing.

Article 23 Adjudicatory personnel shall withdraw on their own initiative under any of the following circumstances, and a party or his statutory agent shall have the right to apply for their withdrawal:
(1) They are a party to the case or a close relative of a party;
(2) They or their close relatives have an interest in the case;
(3) They have served as a witness, expert evaluator, defender, litigation agent, or interpreter in the case;
(4) They have a close relative relationship with the defender or litigation agent of the case;
(5) They have other interests with a party to the case that may affect the impartiality of the trial.

Article 24 Where adjudicatory personnel violate regulations by engaging in any of the following acts, a party or his statutory agent shall have the right to apply for their withdrawal:
(1) Meeting with a party, defender, or litigation agent of the case in violation of regulations;
(2) Recommending or introducing a defender or litigation agent for a party to the case, or introducing the handling of the case to a lawyer or other person;
(3) Soliciting or accepting property or other benefits from a party to the case or his client;
(4) Accepting a banquet hosted by a party to the case or his client, or participating in activities paid for by them;
(5) Borrowing money or goods from a party to the case or his client;
(6) Engaging in other improper acts that may affect the impartiality of the trial.

Article 35 When adjudicating a case, a People's Court shall fully guarantee the right to defense enjoyed by the defendant according to law.
In addition to exercising the right of defense on his own, a defendant may also entrust a defender to defend him. The following persons shall not serve as defenders:
(1) Persons who are currently serving a criminal punishment or are in the probation or parole supervision period;
(2) Persons who have been deprived of or restricted of personal freedom according to law;
(3) Persons who have no capacity or have limited capacity for civil conduct;
(4) Active personnel of a People's Court, People's Procuratorate, public security organ, state security organ, or prison;
(5) People's assessors;
(6) Persons who have an interest in the outcome of the trial of the case; and
(7) Foreigners or stateless persons.
If a person specified in items (4) through (7) of the preceding paragraph is the guardian or a close relative of the defendant and is entrusted by the defendant to serve as a defender, such appointment may be permitted.

Article 42 For any of the following defendants who have not entrusted a defender, the People's Court shall notify a legal aid agency to assign a lawyer to provide them with defense:
(1) A blind, deaf, or mute person;
(2) A mental patient who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his own conduct;
(3) A person who may be sentenced to life imprisonment or the death penalty.
When a Higher People's Court reviews a death sentence case, if the defendant has not entrusted a defender, it shall notify a legal aid agency to assign a lawyer to provide him with defense.

Article 43 Under any of the following circumstances where a defendant has not entrusted a defender, the People's Court may notify a legal aid agency to assign a lawyer to provide him with defense:
(1) In a joint crime case, other defendants have already entrusted defenders;
(2) The case has significant social impact;
(3) The case is one in which the People's Procuratorate has filed a protest;
(4) The defendant's conduct may not constitute a crime; or
(5) Other circumstances where it is necessary to assign a lawyer to provide defense.

Article 64 The facts of a case to be proved by evidence include:
(1) The identity of the defendant and the victim;
(2) Whether the alleged crime exists;
(3) Whether the alleged crime was committed by the defendant;
(4) Whether the defendant has criminal capacity, whether there is culpability, and the motive and purpose of committing the crime;
(5) The time, place, means, consequences of the crime, and the cause of the case, etc.;
(6) The status and role of the defendant in a joint crime;
(7) Whether the defendant has circumstances for heavier, lighter, mitigated, or exempted punishment;
(8) Facts concerning collateral civil actions and the disposition of property involved in the case;
(9) Procedural facts concerning jurisdiction, withdrawal, and adjournment of trial, etc.;
(10) Other facts related to conviction and sentencing.
To find the defendant guilty and impose a heavier punishment on him, the standard of proof that the evidence is reliable and sufficient shall apply.

Article 67 The following persons shall not serve as witnesses to criminal proceedings:
(1) Persons who are physically or mentally defective or are young and thus lack the corresponding ability to discern or to express correctly;
(2) Persons who have an interest in the case and may affect the fair handling of the case;
(3) Personnel of public security and judicial organs or persons employed by them who exercise criminal procedural powers such as inquest, examination, search, and seizure.
Where it is impossible due to objective reasons to have a qualified person serve as a witness, the circumstances shall be noted in the written record, and the relevant activities shall be recorded by video.

Article 69 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on physical evidence and documentary evidence:
(1) Whether the physical evidence or documentary evidence is the original object or original document, whether it has been identified or appraised; whether the photographs, videos, copies of physical evidence, or duplicates, copies of documentary evidence are consistent with the original object or original document, whether they were made by two or more persons, and whether there are textual explanations and signatures of the maker regarding the production process and the location where the original object or document is stored;
(2) Whether the collection procedures and methods of physical evidence and documentary evidence comply with laws and relevant provisions; whether the physical evidence and documentary evidence obtained or seized through inquest, examination, search, or seizure are accompanied by relevant records and lists, whether the records and lists are signed by the investigators, the holder of the item, and the witness, and if the holder of the item has not signed, whether the reason is noted; whether the name, characteristics, quantity, quality, etc., of the item are clearly stated;
(3) Whether the physical evidence or documentary evidence has been damaged or altered during the process of collection, preservation, or appraisal;
(4) Whether the physical evidence or documentary evidence is related to the facts of the case; whether biological samples, traces, and items such as blood stains, body fluids, hair, and fingerprints left at the scene related to the crime and suitable for appraisal have been subjected to DNA identification, fingerprint identification, etc., and compared with the corresponding biological samples, biological characteristics, items, etc., of the defendant or the victim;
(5) Whether all physical evidence and documentary evidence related to the facts of the case have been comprehensively collected.

Article 73 Physical evidence or documentary evidence obtained or seized during an inquest, examination, or search, if not accompanied by records or lists and thus unable to prove the source of the physical or documentary evidence, shall not be used as the basis for adjudication.
Where the collection procedures or methods of physical or documentary evidence have the following defects, they may be adopted after correction or a reasonable explanation is provided:
(1) The records of inquest, examination, search, or seizure or the seizure list are not signed by investigators, the holder of the item, or the witness, or the name, characteristics, quantity, quality, etc., of the item are not clearly stated;
(2) The photographs, videos, or reproductions of physical evidence, or the duplicates or copies of documentary evidence do not indicate that they have been verified as consistent with the original, do not have the time of reproduction, or are not signed or sealed by the person who collected or obtained them;
(3) The photographs, videos, or reproductions of physical evidence, or the duplicates or copies of documentary evidence lack an explanation by the maker regarding the production process and the location of the original object or document, or the explanation is not signed;
(4) There are other defects.
If there are doubts about the source or collection procedures of physical or documentary evidence that cannot be reasonably explained, such physical or documentary evidence shall not be used as the basis for adjudication.

Article 74 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on witness testimony:
(1) Whether the content of the testimony was directly perceived by the witness;
(2) Whether the age, cognitive, memory, and expressive abilities, and physical and mental state of the witness at the time of testifying affect the testimony;
(3) Whether the witness has an interest in the parties or the outcome of the case;
(4) Whether the witness was questioned individually;
(5) Whether the making and modification of the interrogation record comply with laws and relevant provisions, whether the start and end times and location of the interrogation are noted, whether the witness was informed of the rights, obligations, and legal liabilities related to testifying at the first interrogation, and whether the witness has checked and confirmed the interrogation record;
(6) When questioning a minor witness, whether the witness's statutory agent or relevant personnel were notified to be present, and whether such persons were actually present;
(7) Whether the witness testimony was collected through illegal methods such as violence or threats;
(8) Whether the testimonies are mutually corroborated and consistent with other evidence, and whether there are contradictions.

Article 76 Witness testimony shall not be used as the basis for adjudication under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The witness was not questioned individually;
(2) The written testimony was not checked and confirmed by the witness;
(3) When questioning a deaf or mute person, an interpreter proficient in sign language should have been provided but was not;
(4) When questioning a witness who does not understand the local commonly used language or script, an interpreter should have been provided but was not.

Article 77 Where the procedures or methods for collecting witness testimony have the following defects, the testimony may be adopted after correction or a reasonable explanation is provided; if no correction or reasonable explanation can be made, it shall not be used as the basis for adjudication:
(1) The interrogation record does not fill in the names of the interrogator, recorder, statutory agent, or the start and end times and location of the interrogation;
(2) The interrogation location does not comply with the provisions;
(3) The interrogation record does not record that the witness was informed of the rights, obligations, and legal liabilities related to testifying;
(4) The interrogation record reflects that during the same time period, the same interrogator questioned different witnesses.

Article 80 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on the defendant's confessions and excuses:
(1) Whether the time, place, identity and number of interrogators, and methods of interrogation comply with laws and relevant provisions;
(2) Whether the making and modification of the interrogation record comply with laws and relevant provisions, whether the specific start and end times and location of the interrogation are noted, whether the defendant was informed of the relevant rights and legal provisions at the first interrogation, and whether the defendant checked and confirmed the record;
(3) When interrogating a minor defendant, whether the statutory agent or relevant personnel were notified to be present, and whether such persons were actually present;
(4) Whether the defendant's confession was obtained through illegal methods such as extorting confessions by torture;
(5) Whether the defendant's confessions are consistent, whether there are inconsistencies and the reasons for such inconsistencies; whether all of the defendant's confessions and excuses have been transferred with the case file;
(6) Whether the content of the defendant's excuses is consistent with the circumstances of the case and common sense, and whether there are contradictions;
(7) Whether the defendant's confessions and excuses are mutually corroborated and consistent with those of co-defendants and other evidence, and whether there are contradictions.
If necessary, the audio and video recordings of the interrogation process, the health examination records of the defendant when entering and leaving the detention center, and the records may be obtained and used, in conjunction with the recordings, records, and notes, to examine the above content.

Article 81 A defendant's confession shall not be used as the basis for adjudication under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The interrogation record was not checked and confirmed by the defendant;
(2) When interrogating a deaf or mute person, an interpreter proficient in sign language should have been provided but was not;
(3) When interrogating a defendant who does not understand the local commonly used language or script, an interpreter should have been provided but was not.

Article 82 Where the interrogation record has the following defects, it may be adopted after correction or a reasonable explanation is provided; if no correction or reasonable explanation can be made, it shall not be used as the basis for adjudication:
(1) The interrogation time, interrogator, recorder, statutory agent, etc., filled in the interrogation record are incorrect or contradictory;
(2) The interrogator did not sign;
(3) The first interrogation record does not record that the person being interrogated was informed of the relevant rights and legal provisions.

Article 84 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on expert opinions:
(1) Whether the expert institution and the expert have the statutory qualifications;
(2) Whether the expert has circumstances requiring withdrawal;
(3) Whether the source, acquisition, preservation, and submission of the sample for examination comply with laws and relevant provisions, whether they are consistent with the content recorded in the relevant extraction records, seizure lists, etc., and whether the sample is sufficient and reliable;
(4) Whether the formal requirements of the expert opinion are complete, whether it states the reasons for initiating the examination, the client, the expert institution, the examination requirements, the examination process, the examination method, the examination date, etc., and whether it is affixed with the judicial examination special seal by the expert institution and signed and sealed by the expert;
(5) Whether the examination procedure complies with laws and relevant provisions;
(6) Whether the examination process and method comply with the specifications of the relevant profession;
(7) Whether the expert opinion is clear;
(8) Whether the expert opinion is related to the facts to be proved in the case;
(9) Whether the expert opinion contradicts other evidence such as inquest and examination records and related photographs;
(10) Whether the expert opinion was promptly informed to the relevant persons according to law, and whether the parties have any objection to the expert opinion.

Article 85 An expert opinion shall not be used as the basis for adjudication under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The expert institution does not have the statutory qualifications, or the subject of the examination exceeds the business scope or technical conditions of the expert institution;
(2) The expert does not have the statutory qualifications, does not have the relevant professional expertise or title, or has violated the provisions on withdrawal;
(3) The source of the submitted material or sample is unknown, or it does not meet the conditions for examination due to contamination;
(4) The object of examination is inconsistent with the submitted material or sample;
(5) The examination procedure violates the provisions;
(6) The examination process and method do not comply with the specifications of the relevant profession;
(7) The expert opinion document lacks a signature or seal;
(8) The expert opinion is not related to the facts to be proved in the case;
(9) Other circumstances that violate relevant provisions.

Article 88. The following contents shall be emphatically examined in the record of inquest and inspection:
(1) Whether the inquest and inspection are conducted according to law, whether the preparation of the record complies with the law and relevant provisions, and whether the inquest and inspection personnel and witnesses have signed or affixed seals;
(2) Whether the record of inquest and inspection records the cause of initiating the inquest and inspection, the time and place of the inquest and inspection, the persons present, scene orientation, surrounding environment, etc., the positions and characteristics of objects, persons, corpses, etc. at the scene, and the process of inquest, inspection, and search; whether the written record is consistent with the physical objects, drawings, photographs, or video recordings; whether the scene, objects, traces, etc. are forged or damaged; whether physical characteristics, injuries, and physiological conditions are disguised or changed, etc.;
(3) If a supplementary inquest or inspection is conducted, whether the reason for the re-inquest or inspection is explained, and whether the circumstances of the previous and subsequent inquests or inspections are contradictory.

Article 90. The process and method of identification and whether the preparation of the identification record complies with relevant provisions shall be emphatically examined.
An identification record shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The identification is not conducted under the auspices of investigation personnel;
(2) The identifier is allowed to see the identification object before the identification;
(3) The identification activities are not conducted separately;
(4) The identification object is not mixed with other objects having similar characteristics, or the number of objects presented for identification does not comply with the provisions;
(5) Obvious hints are given to the identifier during the identification, or there is obvious suspicion of directed identification;
(6) Other circumstances where relevant provisions are violated and the authenticity of the identification record cannot be determined.

Article 92. The following contents shall be emphatically examined in audio-visual materials:
(1) Whether a description of the extraction process is attached, and whether the source is lawful;
(2) Whether they are originals, and whether there are copies and the number of copies; if they are copies, whether an explanation of the reason for the inability to obtain the originals, the production process of the copies, and the location of the originals is attached, and whether the producer and the original holder of the audio-visual materials have signed or affixed seals;
(3) Whether there are circumstances such as threats or inducement of parties during the production process that violate the law or relevant provisions;
(4) Whether the identities of the producer and holder, and the time, place, conditions, and method of production are indicated;
(5) Whether the content and production process are authentic, and whether there are circumstances such as editing, addition, deletion, or alteration;
(6) Whether the content is related to the facts of the case.
If there is doubt about the audio-visual materials, an expert evaluation shall be conducted.

Article 93. The following contents shall be emphatically examined in electronic data such as emails, electronic data interchange, online chat records, blogs, microblogs, mobile phone messages, electronic signatures, and domain names:
(1) Whether they are transferred together with the original storage medium; when the original storage medium cannot be sealed, is inconvenient to move, or shall be kept, handled, or returned by relevant departments according to law, whether the extraction or copying of electronic data is conducted by two or more persons, whether it is sufficient to ensure the integrity of the electronic data, and whether there is a written description and signatures of the extraction or copying process and the location of the original storage medium;
(2) Whether the collection procedures and methods comply with the law and relevant technical specifications; for electronic data collected through inquest, inspection, search and other investigation activities, whether records and lists are attached and signed by investigation personnel, the electronic data holder, and witnesses; if there is no signature of the holder, whether the reason is indicated; for remotely obtaining electronic data from abroad or another location, whether the relevant circumstances are indicated; whether the specifications, categories, file formats, etc. of the electronic data are clearly indicated;
(3) Whether the content of the electronic data is authentic, and whether there are circumstances such as deletion, modification, or addition;
(4) Whether the electronic data is related to the facts of the case;
(5) Whether the electronic data related to the facts of the case is comprehensively collected.
If there is doubt about the electronic data, an expert evaluation or examination shall be conducted.

Article 94. Audio-visual materials or electronic data shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Their authenticity cannot be determined after examination;
(2) There is doubt about the time, place, method, etc. of their production or acquisition, and the necessary proof cannot be provided or a reasonable explanation cannot be made.

Article 103. Under any of the following circumstances, the second instance People's Court shall examine the legality of evidence collection and handle the matter in accordance with the Criminal Procedure Law and the relevant provisions of this Interpretation:
(1) The first instance People's Court did not examine the application of the party, his or her defender, or litigation agent to exclude illegally obtained evidence, and used such evidence as the basis for deciding the case;
(2) The People's Procuratorate or the defendant, the private prosecutor, or his or her statutory agent, dissatisfied with the investigation conclusion made by the first instance People's Court concerning the legality of evidence collection, files a protest or appeal;
(3) The party, his or her defender, or litigation agent discovers relevant clues or materials only after the conclusion of the first instance and applies to the People's Court to exclude illegally obtained evidence.

Article 105. Where there is no direct evidence, but the circumstantial evidence concurrently meets the following conditions, the defendant may be found guilty:
(1) The evidence has been verified as true;
(2) The evidence corroborates each other, and there are no irresolvable contradictions or inexplicable doubts;
(3) All evidence in the case has formed a complete proof system;
(4) Determining the facts of the case based on the evidence is sufficient to exclude reasonable doubt, and the conclusion is unique;
(5) The reasoning using the evidence conforms to logic and experience.

Article 109. The following evidence shall be used with caution, and may be accepted if corroborated by other evidence:
(1) Statements, testimonies, and confessions made by victims, witnesses, or defendants who have physical or mental defects, have some difficulty in perceiving or expressing the facts of the case, but have not yet lost the ability to correctly perceive and express;
(2) Testimonies favorable to the defendant made by witnesses who have a familial relationship or other close relationship with the defendant, or testimonies unfavorable to the defendant made by witnesses who have a conflict of interest with the defendant.

Article 117. Where release on bail pending investigation or trial is decided for a defendant falling under any of the following categories, the defendant may be ordered to provide one or two guarantors:
(1) The defendant is unable to pay the deposit;
(2) The defendant is a minor or has reached the age of seventy-five;
(3) Other defendants for whom it is inappropriate to collect a deposit.

Article 129 Where a defendant released on bail pending investigation or trial falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall decide to arrest:
(1) intentionally committing a new crime;
(2) attempting suicide or escape;
(3) destroying or forging evidence, interfering with witness testimony or colluding to make false statements;
(4) retaliating against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(5) failing to appear in court without justifiable reasons after being summoned, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings;
(6) arbitrarily changing contact information or place of residence, resulting in inability to summon, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings;
(7) leaving the city or county where he or she resides without approval, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has, on two occasions, left the city or county where he or she resides without approval;
(8) entering a specific place, meeting or communicating with specific persons, or engaging in specific activities in violation of regulations, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has on two occasions violated the relevant provisions;
(9) any other circumstance under which an arrest shall be decided according to law.

Article 130 Where a defendant under residential surveillance falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall decide to arrest:
(1) falling under any of the circumstances specified in items (1) through (5) of the preceding Article;
(2) leaving the place where residential surveillance is executed without approval, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has, on two occasions, left the place where residential surveillance is executed without approval;
(3) meeting or communicating with others without approval, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has, on two occasions, met or communicated with others without approval;
(4) in the case of a defendant who was not arrested due to serious illness or inability to take care of himself or herself, or due to pregnancy or being currently nursing an infant, the illness has been cured or the nursing period has expired;
(5) any other circumstance under which an arrest shall be decided according to law.

Article 133 Where an arrested defendant falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may change the compulsory measure:
(1) suffering from a serious illness and being unable to take care of himself or herself;
(2) being pregnant or currently nursing an infant;
(3) being the sole supporter of a person who is unable to take care of himself or herself.

Article 134 Where a court of first instance sentences a defendant to be not guilty, not to bear criminal liability, or to be exempted from criminal punishment, and the defendant is in custody, the defendant shall be released immediately after the pronouncement of judgment.
Where an arrested defendant falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall change the compulsory measure or release the defendant:
(1) the court of first instance imposes public surveillance, pronounces a suspended sentence, or imposes solely a supplementary punishment, and the judgment has not yet become legally effective;
(2) the period during which the defendant has been held in custody has reached the term of the sentence imposed by the court of first instance;
(3) the case cannot be concluded within the time limit prescribed by law.

Article 143 Persons who are liable for compensation in an incidental civil action according to law include:
(1) the criminal defendant and other joint infringers who are not subject to criminal liability;
(2) the guardian of the criminal defendant;
(3) the heir to the estate of a criminal sentenced to death;
(4) the heir to the estate of a defendant in a joint crime case who dies before the conclusion of the case;
(5) other entities or individuals that shall bear compensation liability for the victim's material losses according to law.
Where a friend or relative of the defendant in an incidental civil action voluntarily pays compensation on behalf of the defendant, such compensation shall be permitted.

Article 145 The conditions for bringing an incidental civil action are:
(1) the plaintiff meets the statutory conditions;
(2) there is a clear defendant;
(3) there are specific claims for compensation and the facts and reasons therefor;
(4) the case falls within the scope of incidental civil actions accepted by the People's Court.

Article 180 For a case in which a public prosecution has been initiated, after receiving the indictment (eight copies, plus five copies for each additional defendant) and the case file and evidence, the People's Court shall designate an adjudicator to examine the following:
(1) whether the case is within the jurisdiction of that court;
(2) whether the indictment states the identity of the defendant, whether the defendant has been or is currently subject to criminal punishment, the type of compulsory measures taken, the place of custody, the time, place, means, and consequences of the crime, and other circumstances that may affect conviction and sentencing;
(3) whether evidence materials proving the alleged criminal facts have been transferred, including the approval decision for technical investigation measures and the evidence materials collected;
(4) whether the defendant's illegal gains or other property involved in the case have been sealed, seized, or frozen, and whether evidence materials proving that such property shall be recovered according to law are attached;
(5) whether the name, address, and contact information of the victim are listed; whether a list of witnesses and expert evaluators is attached; whether there is a request for the court to notify witnesses, expert evaluators, and persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court, listing the names, gender, age, occupation, address, and contact information of such persons; whether a list of witnesses, expert evaluators, and victims requiring protection is attached;
(6) where a party has retained a defender or litigation agent, or has accepted legal aid, whether the names, addresses, and contact information of the defender and litigation agent are listed;
(7) whether an incidental civil action has been initiated; if an incidental civil action has been initiated, whether the names, addresses, and contact information of the parties to the incidental civil action are listed and whether relevant evidence materials are attached;
(8) whether the various legal procedures and litigation documents of the investigation and examination for prosecution stages are complete;
(9) whether there are circumstances under which criminal liability shall not be investigated as provided in paragraphs 2 through 6 of Article 15 of the Criminal Procedure Law.

Article 181 After examining a case in which a public prosecution has been initiated, the People's Court shall handle it according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the case is one that shall only be handled upon complaint, the court shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate and inform the victim of the right to initiate a private prosecution;
(2) if the case is not within its jurisdiction or the defendant is not present, the court shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate;
(3) if the case does not meet any of the requirements under paragraphs 2 through 8 of the preceding article and supplementary materials are needed, the court shall notify the People's Procuratorate to supplement the materials within three days;
(4) if, after a defendant is acquitted in accordance with paragraph 3 of Article 195 of the Criminal Procedure Law, the People's Procuratorate re-institutes prosecution based on new facts or evidence, the court shall accept the case according to law;
(5) if a case has been permitted to withdraw the prosecution by ruling in accordance with Article 242 of this Interpretation, and the People's Procuratorate re-institutes prosecution without new facts or evidence, the court shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate;
(6) if the circumstances under paragraphs 2 through 6 of Article 15 of the Criminal Procedure Law are present, the court shall rule to terminate the adjudication or return the case to the People's Procuratorate;
(7) if the true identity of the defendant is unknown but the conditions under paragraph 2 of Article 158 of the Criminal Procedure Law are met, the court shall accept the case according to law.
The examination of whether to accept a public prosecution case shall be completed within seven days.

Article 182 Before the trial session begins, the People's Court shall carry out the following:
(1) determine the presiding judge and the composition of the collegial panel;
(2) serve a copy of the indictment on the defendant and defender ten days before the trial session;
(3) notify the parties, legal representatives, defenders, and litigation agents to provide, five days before the trial session, the list of witnesses and expert evaluators as well as the evidence intended to be presented in court; if applying for witnesses, expert evaluators, or persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court, the names, gender, age, occupation, address, and contact information of such persons shall be listed;
(4) notify the People's Procuratorate of the time and place of the trial session three days before the trial session;
(5) serve the summons to the parties and the notices to the defenders, litigation agents, legal representatives, witnesses, expert evaluators, and others to appear in court three days before the trial session; such notices may also be sent by telephone, text message, fax, email, or other means that can confirm receipt;
(6) in cases to be tried in public, announce the cause of action, defendant's name, trial time, and place three days before the trial session.
The above work shall be recorded in the case file.

Article 183 If a case has any of the following circumstances, the adjudicators may convene a pre-trial conference:
(1) a party, its defender, or litigation agent applies to exclude illegally obtained evidence;
(2) there is a large amount of evidence materials and the case is major and complex;
(3) the case has significant social impact;
(4) other circumstances requiring a pre-trial conference.
When convening a pre-trial conference, the defendant may be notified to participate depending on the circumstances of the case.

Article 184 When convening a pre-trial conference, the adjudicators may inquire of the prosecution and the defense about the following issues and hear their opinions:
(1) whether there is any objection to the jurisdiction of the case;
(2) whether an application is made for the withdrawal of relevant personnel;
(3) whether an application is made to obtain evidence materials collected by the public security organ or the People's Procuratorate during investigation or examination for prosecution but not transferred with the case file that prove the defendant's innocence or lesser guilt;
(4) whether new evidence will be provided;
(5) whether there is any objection to the list of witnesses, expert evaluators, or persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court;
(6) whether an application is made to exclude illegally obtained evidence;
(7) whether an application is made for closed trial;
(8) other issues related to the trial.
The adjudicators may ask the prosecution and defense whether they have any objections to the evidence materials; for evidence objected to, the court shall focus its investigation during the trial; for evidence not objected to, the presentation and examination of evidence during trial may be simplified.
If the victim or his or her legal representative or near relative files an incidental civil action, mediation may be conducted.
A record shall be made of the pre-trial conference.

Article 185 Before the trial session begins, the collegial panel may prepare a trial outline, which generally includes the following:
(1) the division of duties among the members of the collegial panel during trial;
(2) the key points of the criminal facts alleged in the indictment and the main points for determining the nature of the case;
(3) the main points of the case facts that need to be understood when interrogating the defendant;
(4) the list of witnesses, expert evaluators, persons with specialized knowledge, and investigators appearing in court;
(5) the catalog of evidence that the prosecution and defense apply to present in court;
(6) potential issues that may arise during trial and countermeasures.

Article 189 Before the trial session begins, the court clerk shall carry out the following in order:
(1) upon the entrustment of the presiding judge, ascertain whether the public prosecutor, parties, witnesses, and other litigation participants are present;
(2) read the court rules;
(3) invite the public prosecutor and relevant litigation participants to enter the courtroom;
(4) invite the presiding judge, judges (people's assessors) to enter the courtroom;
(5) after the adjudicators are seated, report to the presiding judge that preparations before trial are complete.

Article 190 After the presiding judge announces the opening of the trial session and summons the defendant into court, he or she shall ascertain the following about the defendant:
(1) the defendant's name, date of birth, ethnicity, place of birth, education level, occupation, address, or the name and domicile of the defendant entity, and the name and position of its litigation representative;
(2) whether the defendant has received any legal sanction and the type and time of such sanction;
(3) whether compulsory measures have been taken and the type and time of such measures;
(4) the date of receipt of the copy of the indictment; if there is an incidental civil action, the date on which the defendant in the incidental civil action received the incidental civil complaint.
If there are many defendants, the above may be ascertained before the trial session, but the presiding judge shall explain this at the opening of the trial.

Article 193 The presiding judge shall inform the parties, their legal representatives, defenders, and litigation agents that during the court proceedings they are entitled according to law to the following procedural rights:
(1) they may apply for the withdrawal of a member of the collegial panel, the court clerk, the public prosecutor, an expert evaluator, or an interpreter;
(2) they may present evidence, apply for notification of new witnesses to appear in court, obtain new evidence, or apply for a new expert evaluation or inspection/examination;
(3) the defendant may conduct his or her own defense;
(4) the defendant may make a final statement after the conclusion of the court debate.

Article 206 If a witness is unable to testify in court due to any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may permit him or her not to appear in court:
(1) suffering from a serious illness or having great difficulty with mobility during the trial period;
(2) residing far from the trial location and having extremely inconvenient transportation;
(3) being abroad and unable to return within a short period;
(4) having other objective reasons that genuinely prevent him or her from appearing in court.
In the circumstances specified in the preceding paragraph, the witness may testify by video or other means.

Article 213 Questioning of a witness shall follow these rules:
(1) the content of the question shall be related to the facts of the case;
(2) leading questions shall not be used;
(3) the witness shall not be threatened;
(4) the dignity of the witness shall not be impaired.
The provisions of the preceding paragraph apply to the interrogation or questioning of the defendant, victim, parties to an incidental civil action, expert evaluators, and persons with specialized knowledge.

Article 225 During trial, facts and evidence related to sentencing shall be investigated.
In addition to examining whether the defendant has statutory sentencing circumstances, the People's Court shall also examine the following circumstances affecting sentencing based on the case:
(1) The cause of the case;
(2) Whether the victim is at fault and the degree of fault, and whether the victim bears responsibility for the escalation of the conflict and the extent of such responsibility;
(3) Whether the defendant's close relatives assisted in apprehending the defendant;
(4) The defendant's usual conduct and whether the defendant shows remorse;
(5) The return of stolen goods, restitution, and compensation;
(6) Whether the defendant has obtained forgiveness from the victim or the victim's close relatives;
(7) Other circumstances affecting sentencing.

Article 229 Court debate shall be conducted under the direction of the presiding judge in the following order:
(1) Statement by the public prosecutor;
(2) Statement by the victim and the victim's litigation agent;
(3) Self-defense by the defendant;
(4) Defense by the defender;
(5) Debate between the prosecution and the defense.

Article 241 For first-instance public prosecution cases, after trial, the People's Court shall render a judgment or ruling according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts charged in the indictment are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the crime charged against the defendant is established pursuant to law, a guilty verdict shall be rendered;
(2) If the facts charged in the indictment are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, but the charged crime is inconsistent with the crime determined through trial, a guilty verdict shall be rendered based on the crime determined through trial;
(3) If the facts of the case are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the defendant is found not guilty pursuant to law, a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant;
(4) If the evidence is insufficient and the defendant cannot be found guilty, a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant on the grounds of insufficient evidence and that the charged crime cannot be established;
(5) If part of the facts of the case are clear and the evidence is reliable and sufficient, a guilty or not guilty verdict shall be rendered for that part; for the part where the facts are unclear or evidence is insufficient, no finding shall be made;
(6) If the defendant is under the age of 16 and is not subject to criminal punishment, a judgment shall be rendered declaring that the defendant bears no criminal liability;
(7) If the defendant is a mental patient who caused harmful consequences when unable to recognize or control his or her own conduct, and is not subject to criminal punishment, a judgment shall be rendered declaring that the defendant bears no criminal liability;
(8) If the statute of limitations for prosecution has expired and prosecution is not mandatory, or if the punishment has been remitted by a special amnesty order, a ruling shall be rendered to terminate the trial;
(9) If the defendant has died, a ruling shall be rendered to terminate the trial; however, if based on the facts of the case that have been ascertained and the evidence that has been determined, the defendant can be confirmed to be not guilty, a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant.
Where the circumstances provided for in subparagraph (2) of the preceding paragraph exist, the People's Court shall, before rendering the judgment, hear the opinions of both the prosecution and the defense, and ensure that the defendant and defender fully exercise the right of defense. If necessary, the court may reopen the trial and organize the prosecution and defense to debate over what crime the defendant's conduct constitutes.

Article 249 During trial, participants in the proceedings and spectators shall observe the following discipline:
(1) Comply with the direction of the court and observe courtroom etiquette;
(2) Refrain from applauding, making noise, causing disturbance, or walking around arbitrarily;
(3) Refrain from making audio recordings, video recordings, or photographs of the trial activities, or disseminating the trial situation by sending emails, blogs, microblogs, or other means, except for journalists permitted by the People's Court;
(4) Spectators shall not speak or ask questions;
(5) Refrain from committing any other acts that disrupt courtroom order.

Article 250 During trial, if a participant in the proceedings or a spectator disrupts courtroom order, the presiding judge shall handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the circumstances are relatively minor, the presiding judge shall give a warning to stop the conduct and administer a reprimand;
(2) If the person refuses to stop after being warned, the presiding judge may instruct a judicial police officer to forcibly remove the person from the courtroom;
(3) If the circumstances are serious, upon approval by the president of the court, the person may be fined not more than 1,000 yuan or detained for not more than 15 days;
(4) If the person makes audio recordings, video recordings, or photographs without permission, or disseminates the trial situation by sending emails, blogs, microblogs, or other means, the storage medium or relevant equipment may be temporarily seized.
If a participant in the proceedings or spectator disagrees with the decision of a fine or detention, the person may directly apply for reconsideration to the People's Court at the next higher level, or may apply for reconsideration through the People's Court that made the decision on the fine or detention. If the person applies for reconsideration through the People's Court that made the decision, that People's Court shall, within three days from receipt of the reconsideration application, submit the reconsideration application, the decision on fine or detention, and the relevant facts and evidentiary materials to the People's Court at the next higher level for reconsideration. During the period of reconsideration, the execution of the decision shall not be suspended.

Article 259 When a People's Court accepts a private prosecution case, it shall satisfy the following conditions:
(1) It complies with the provisions of Article 204 of the Criminal Procedure Law and Article 1 of this Interpretation;
(2) It falls within the jurisdiction of this court;
(3) The victim files a complaint;
(4) There is a clearly identified defendant, specific claims, and evidence proving the defendant's criminal facts.

Article 262 A written criminal complaint shall include the following:
(1) The name, sex, age, ethnicity, place of birth, level of education, occupation, work unit, address, and contact information of the private prosecutor (or the person filing the complaint on behalf of the victim) and the defendant;
(2) The time, place, means, circumstances, and harmful consequences of the defendant's commission of the crime, etc.;
(3) The specific claims;
(4) The People's Court to which it is addressed and the date of submission;
(5) The name, source, etc. of evidence;
(6) The name, address, contact information, etc. of witnesses.
If a complaint is filed against two or more defendants, copies of the written criminal complaint shall be provided according to the number of defendants.

Article 263 For a private prosecution case, the People's Court shall complete its examination within fifteen days. Upon examination, if the conditions for acceptance are satisfied, it shall decide to open a case and notify the private prosecutor or the person filing the complaint on behalf of the victim in writing.
Under any of the following circumstances, the court shall persuade the private prosecutor to withdraw the prosecution; if the private prosecutor does not withdraw the prosecution, a ruling shall be made not to accept the case:
(1) The case is not one of those provided for in Article 1 of this Interpretation;
(2) There is insufficient evidence of the crime;
(3) The statute of limitations for prosecution has expired for the crime;
(4) The defendant has died;
(5) The defendant's whereabouts are unknown;
(6) Except for withdrawals due to insufficient evidence, the private prosecutor files a complaint again regarding the same facts after withdrawing the previous complaint;
(7) After the case is closed through mediation by the People's Court, the private prosecutor repudiates the mediation and files a complaint again regarding the same facts.

Article 277 In a case that is to be handled only upon complaint or a minor criminal case for which the victim has evidence to prove, the defendant or his or her legal representative may, during the proceedings, file a counterclaim against the private prosecutor. The counterclaim shall satisfy the following conditions:
(1) The object of the counterclaim must be the private prosecutor in the current case;
(2) The content of the counterclaim must be an act related to the current case;
(3) The case of the counterclaim must comply with the provisions of Items (1) and (2) of Article 1 of this Interpretation.
The provisions on private prosecution cases shall apply to counterclaim cases, which shall be tried together with the private prosecution case. The withdrawal of the private prosecution by the private prosecutor shall not affect the continued trial of the counterclaim case.

Article 290 Under any of the following circumstances, the summary procedure shall not apply:
(1) The defendant is blind, deaf, or mute;
(2) The defendant is a person with mental illness who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his or her own conduct;
(3) The case has significant social impact;
(4) In a joint crime case, some defendants do not confess or object to the application of the summary procedure;
(5) The defense counsel makes a defense of not guilty;
(6) The defendant confesses but, upon examination, it is considered that the conduct may not constitute a crime;
(7) Other circumstances where it is inappropriate to apply the summary procedure.

Article 295 When trying a case under the summary procedure, the court session may be simplified as follows:
(1) The public prosecutor may read the bill of prosecution in summary;
(2) The questioning and interrogation of the defendant by the public prosecutor, defense counsel, and judicial officers may be simplified or omitted;
(3) For evidence on which the prosecution and defense have no objection, a statement may be made only as to the name of the evidence and the matters it proves; for evidence on which the prosecution and defense have objection, or which the court considers necessary to investigate and verify, it shall be presented and subjected to cross-examination;
(4) If the prosecution and defense have no objection to the facts and evidence related to conviction and sentencing, the court trial may directly focus on the determination of the charge and the issue of sentencing.
When trying a case under the summary procedure, the defendant's final statement shall be heard before the judgment is pronounced.

Article 298 When trying a case under the summary procedure, if any of the following circumstances occurs during the court trial, the case shall be transferred to the ordinary procedure:
(1) The defendant's conduct may not constitute a crime;
(2) The defendant may not bear criminal liability;
(3) The defendant denies the criminal facts alleged in the prosecution in court;
(4) The facts of the case are unclear and the evidence is insufficient;
(5) Other circumstances where the summary procedure should not or is not appropriate to be applied.
For a case transferred to the ordinary procedure, the trial period shall be calculated from the date of the decision to transfer to the ordinary procedure.

Article 309 The People's Court of second instance shall examine whether the case file and evidence for the appeal or protest transferred by the People's Court of first instance include the following:
(1) The letter of transfer of the appeal or protest case;
(2) The petition for appeal or the written protest;
(3) Eight copies of the first-instance judgment or ruling (with one additional copy for each additional defendant) and the electronic text thereof;
(4) All case files and evidence, including the trial report and other materials that shall be transferred.
If the materials listed in the preceding paragraph are complete, the People's Court of second instance shall accept the case; if the materials are incomplete, it shall notify the People's Court of first instance to supplement them promptly.

Article 315 In cases of appeal or protest, review shall focus on the following:
(1) Whether the facts determined in the first instance judgment are clear and the evidence is reliable and sufficient;
(2) Whether the law applied in the first instance judgment is correct and the sentence is appropriate;
(3) Whether there are any circumstances violating statutory procedural rules during investigation, examination and prosecution, or first instance proceedings;
(4) Whether the appeal or protest raises new facts or evidence;
(5) The statements and explanations of the defendant;
(6) The defense opinions of the defender and whether they have been adopted;
(7) Whether the judgment or ruling on the collateral civil part is lawful and appropriate;
(8) The opinions discussed by the collegial panel and the adjudication committee of the people's court of first instance.

Article 317 The following cases shall be tried in open court in accordance with Article 223(1) of the Criminal Procedure Law:
(1) Appeal cases where the defendant, private prosecutor or their legal representatives challenge the facts or evidence determined in the first instance, which may affect the conviction and sentencing;
(2) Appeal cases where the defendant has been sentenced to immediate execution of death;
(3) Cases protested by the People's Procuratorate;
(4) Other cases that shall be tried in open court.
Where a defendant sentenced to immediate execution of death does not appeal but other co-defendants in the same case file an appeal, the people's court of second instance shall try the case in open court.
For appeal cases where the defendant has been sentenced to death with a two-year reprieve, although they do not fall under the circumstance in paragraph (1) of the first clause, they shall also be tried in open court where conditions permit.

Article 322 Where a case of appeal or protest is tried in open court, in addition to applying the relevant provisions of first instance procedures by reference, it shall be conducted in accordance with the following:
(1) During the court investigation stage, after the adjudicators read out the first instance judgment or ruling, in an appeal case, the appellant or defender shall first read out the petition of appeal or state the grounds for appeal; in a protest case, the procurator shall first read out the protest petition; in a case where there is both an appeal and a protest, the procurator shall first read out the protest petition, and then the appellant or defender shall read out the petition of appeal or state the grounds for appeal;
(2) During the court debate stage, in an appeal case, the appellant and defender shall first make statements, followed by the procurator and litigation agent; in a protest case, the procurator and litigation agent shall first make statements, followed by the defendant and defender; in a case where there is both an appeal and a protest, the procurator and litigation agent shall first make statements, followed by the appellant and defender.

Article 323 Where a case of appeal or protest is tried in open court, the trial may focus on the issues disputed or the parts with doubts concerning the first instance judgment or ruling. Depending on the circumstances of the case, the trial may be conducted in the following manner:
(1) When reading out the first instance judgment, only the cause of action, main facts, names of evidence, and the holding of the judgment may be read;
(2) The court investigation shall focus on the facts and evidence challenged with respect to the first instance judgment, as well as new evidence submitted; facts, evidence, and circumstances that are not disputed may be directly confirmed;
(3) For co-defendants in the same case who have not appealed and who are not requested to appear in court or whose appearance is deemed unnecessary by the people's court, they need no longer be summoned to appear;
(4) In a case where the defendant has committed multiple crimes, those crimes for which the facts are clear and undisputed may not be tried during the court session.
In cases tried jointly, if a defendant who has not appealed and against whom the People's Procuratorate has not protested requests to appear in court, such request shall be granted. The defendant who appears in court may participate in the court investigation and debate.

Article 325 Where a case is appealed by the defendant or his legal representative, defender, or close relative, the sentence of the defendant shall not be aggravated, and the following provisions shall be implemented:
(1) In a case tried jointly, if only some of the defendants appeal, neither the sentence of the appellant nor the sentence of other co-defendants in the same case shall be aggravated;
(2) If the facts determined in the original judgment are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, but the crime charged is improper, the charge may be changed, but the sentence shall not be aggravated;
(3) If the original judgment imposes combined punishment for multiple crimes against the defendant, neither the sentence to be enforced nor the sentence for any of the individual crimes shall be aggravated;
(4) If the original judgment imposes probation on the defendant, the probation shall not be revoked or the probation period extended;
(5) If the original judgment did not impose a restraining order, no such order shall be added; if the original judgment imposed a restraining order, its content shall not be added to or its duration extended;
(6) If the original judgment imposed a death sentence with a two-year reprieve on the defendant without restricting commutation of punishment, commutation shall not be restricted;
(7) If the facts determined in the original judgment are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, but the sentence imposed is manifestly lenient and supplementary punishments that should have been applied were not applied, the sentence shall not be directly aggravated or supplementary punishments imposed, nor shall the case be remanded to the people's court of first instance for retrial on the ground of unclear facts or insufficient evidence. If a modification of the judgment is necessary according to law, it shall be done through retrial under the trial supervision procedure after the second instance judgment or ruling takes effect.
Cases protested by the People's Procuratorate or appealed by the private prosecutor shall not be subject to the restrictions in the preceding paragraph.

Article 336 Cases submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval of a sentence below the statutory punishment shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) Where the defendant has not appealed and the People's Procuratorate has not protested, the case shall be submitted to the people's court at the next higher level for review within three days after the expiration of the appeal or protest period. If the people's court at the next higher level agrees with the original judgment, it shall report level by level in writing to the Supreme People's Court for approval; if it disagrees, it shall rule to remand the case for retrial, or change the jurisdiction and retry the case under first instance procedures. If the original judgment was rendered by a Primary People's Court, the Higher People's Court may designate an Intermediate People's Court to retry the case under first instance procedures;
(2) Where the defendant appeals or the People's Procuratorate protests, the case shall be adjudicated under second instance procedures. If the second instance upholds the original judgment, or after modification the sentence is still below the statutory punishment, the case shall be reported level by level to the Supreme People's Court for approval in accordance with the preceding subparagraph.

Article 341 Cases submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval of parole that is not limited by the executed term of sentence because the criminal has special circumstances shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) After the Intermediate People's Court renders a parole ruling according to law, it shall submit the ruling to the Higher People's Court for review. If the Higher People's Court agrees, it shall submit a written request to the Supreme People's Court for approval; if it disagrees, it shall rule to revoke the parole ruling of the Intermediate People's Court;
(2) After the Higher People's Court renders a parole ruling according to law, it shall submit the ruling to the Supreme People's Court for approval.

Article 344 Cases submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval of a death sentence shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) Where, in a first instance case in which the Intermediate People's Court imposes a death sentence, the defendant has not appealed and the People's Procuratorate has not protested, the case shall be submitted to the Higher People's Court for review within ten days after the expiration of the appeal or protest period. If the Higher People's Court agrees to the death sentence, it shall submit the case to the Supreme People's Court for approval within ten days after rendering its ruling; if it disagrees, it shall bring the case up for trial under second instance procedures or remand it for retrial;
(2) Where, in a first instance case in which the Intermediate People's Court imposes a death sentence, the defendant appeals or the People's Procuratorate protests, and the Higher People's Court rules to uphold the sentence, it shall submit the case to the Supreme People's Court for approval within ten days after rendering its ruling;
(3) Where, in a first instance case in which the Higher People's Court imposes a death sentence, the defendant has not appealed and the People's Procuratorate has not protested, the case shall be submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval within ten days after the expiration of the appeal or protest period.
The Higher People's Court, when reviewing a death sentence case, shall interrogate the defendant.

Article 347 The report requesting review shall specify the cause of action, a brief account of the case, the trial process, and the outcome of the judgment.
The comprehensive report on the death sentence case shall include the following:
(1) Basic information of the defendant and the victim. If the defendant has a prior criminal record or has previously received administrative penalties, this shall be stated;
(2) The origin and trial process of the case. If the case was previously remanded for retrial, the reason, time, and case number of the remand shall be stated;
(3) The circumstances of the case detection. If the defendant was captured or the case was solved through technical investigation measures, as well as circumstances related to the determination of voluntary surrender or meritorious service, these shall be stated;
(4) The circumstances of the first instance trial, including the opinions of the prosecution and defense, the criminal facts determined in the first instance, and the opinions of the collegial panel and the adjudication committee;
(5) The circumstances of the second instance trial or the review by the Higher People's Court, including the grounds of appeal, the opinions of the procuratorial organ, the facts determined in the second instance trial or the review by the Higher People's Court, the evidence admitted and the reasons therefor, and the opinions of the prosecution and defense and whether they were adopted;
(6) Issues requiring explanation, including the conviction and sentencing of co-defendants who were handled separately in the joint crime case, whether the case has significant social impact, and the reactions of the parties;
(7) The proposed disposition, setting out the opinions of the collegial panel and the adjudication committee.

Article 348 When reviewing a death sentence or death sentence with a two-year reprieve case, the following shall be comprehensively reviewed:
(1) The age of the defendant, whether the defendant has criminal capacity, and whether the defendant is a pregnant woman;
(2) Whether the facts determined in the original judgment are clear and the evidence is reliable and sufficient;
(3) The circumstances, consequences, and degree of harm of the crime;
(4) Whether the law applied in the original judgment is correct, whether a death sentence is mandatory, and whether immediate execution is mandatory;
(5) Whether there are statutory or discretionary circumstances for heavier, lighter, or mitigated punishment;
(6) Whether the procedural rules are lawful;
(7) Other circumstances that shall be reviewed.

Article 349 The Higher People's Court, when reviewing a death sentence with a two-year reprieve case, shall handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts determined in the original judgment and the law applied are correct, the sentence is appropriate, and the procedural rules are lawful, a ruling of approval shall be issued;
(2) If there are defects in a specific fact determined or a statutory provision cited in the original judgment, but the imposition of a death sentence with a two-year reprieve on the defendant is not improper, a judgment or ruling of approval may be issued after correction;
(3) If the facts determined in the original judgment are correct, but the law applied is erroneous or the sentence is excessively severe, the judgment shall be modified;
(4) If the facts in the original judgment are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, a ruling of disapproval may be issued, setting aside the original judgment and remanding the case for retrial, or modifying the judgment according to law;
(5) If new facts or evidence affecting conviction and sentencing emerge during the review period, a ruling of disapproval may be issued, setting aside the original judgment and remanding the case for retrial, or modifying the judgment according to law after trial in accordance with Article 220 of this Interpretation;
(6) If the original trial violated statutory procedural rules, which may affect a fair trial, a ruling of disapproval shall be issued, setting aside the original judgment and remanding the case for retrial.
The Higher People's Court, when reviewing a death sentence with a two-year reprieve case, shall not aggravate the defendant's sentence.

Article 350: When the Supreme People's Court reviews a death penalty case, it shall handle the case according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts determined by the original judgment and the application of law are correct, the punishment is appropriate, and the litigation procedure is lawful, it shall rule to approve;
(2) If there are flaws in a specific fact determined by the original judgment or in the cited legal provisions, but imposing the death penalty on the defendant is not improper, it may, after correction, render a judgment or ruling of approval;
(3) If the facts of the original judgment are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, it shall rule not to approve, revoke the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(4) If new facts or evidence affecting the determination of guilt and punishment emerge during the review period, it shall rule not to approve, revoke the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(5) If the facts determined by the original judgment are correct but the death penalty should not have been imposed according to law, it shall rule not to approve, revoke the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(6) If the original trial violated the statutory litigation procedure and may affect a fair trial, it shall rule not to approve, revoke the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial.

Article 363: For tangible objects that are not suitable for transfer, the following contents shall be examined separately according to the circumstances:
(1) For bulk items or items not easy to transport, whether the authority that sealed up or seized them transferred with the case the list of sealed-up or seized items, accompanied by photographs of the original items and the seal-up formalities, and indicated the storage location, etc.;
(2) For items subject to decay, mildew, or not easy to preserve, whether after the authority that sealed up or seized them sold them, it transferred with the case the photographs of the original items, the list, and the vouchers (copies) for the disposal of the converted price, etc.;
(3) For firearms, ammunition, highly toxic substances, flammable and explosive substances, and other contraband or dangerous items, whether after the authority that sealed up or seized them handled them in accordance with relevant provisions, it transferred with the case the photographs of the original items and the list, etc.
For the aforesaid tangible objects not suitable for transfer, if appraisal or valuation is required according to law, it shall also be examined whether appraisal or valuation opinions are attached.
For currency, negotiable securities, etc., that have been sealed up or seized but not transferred, it shall be examined whether photographs of the original items, a list, or other supporting documents are attached.

Article 372: To file a petition with a people's court, the following materials shall be submitted:
(1) A petition letter, which shall state the basic information of the party, contact information, and the facts and reasons for the petition;
(2) The original first-instance and second-instance judgments, rulings, and other legal documents. If the people's court has already conducted a review or retrial, a notice of rejection, a retrial decision, a retrial judgment, or ruling shall be attached;
(3) Other relevant materials. If the petition is based on new evidence proving that the facts determined by the original judgment or ruling are indeed erroneous, relevant evidentiary materials shall be attached at the same time; if the petitioner requests the people's court to investigate and collect evidence, relevant clues or materials shall be attached.
If the petition does not comply with the provisions of the preceding paragraph, the people's court shall inform the petitioner to supplement the materials; if the petitioner refuses to supplement the necessary materials without justifiable reasons, the petition shall not be examined.

Article 375: For a petition case placed on file for examination, a decision shall be made within three months, and in no case shall it exceed six months.
If, after examination, any of the following circumstances exists, a decision shall be made to retry the case in accordance with the provisions of Article 242 of the Criminal Procedure Law:
(1) There is new evidence proving that the facts determined by the original judgment or ruling are indeed erroneous, which may affect the determination of guilt and punishment;
(2) The evidence on which the conviction and sentencing were based is not reliable, insufficient, and should be excluded according to law;
(3) There are contradictions among the main evidence proving the facts of the case;
(4) The main factual basis has been changed or revoked according to law;
(5) The crime was wrongly determined;
(6) The punishment is obviously inappropriate;
(7) The provisions of the law on retroactivity have been violated;
(8) The litigation procedure provided by law has been violated, which may affect a fair judgment;
(9) The judicial officers engaged in conduct such as embezzlement, bribery, favoritism, or perversion of the law during the trial of the case.
If the petition does not fall under any of the above circumstances, the petitioner shall be persuaded to withdraw the petition; if the petitioner still insists on the petition, a written notice of rejection shall be served.

Article 376: Evidence that falls under any of the following circumstances and may change the facts on which the original judgment or ruling was based for conviction and sentencing shall be deemed as "new evidence" as provided in paragraph (1) of Article 242 of the Criminal Procedure Law:
(1) Evidence newly discovered after the original judgment or ruling took effect;
(2) Evidence discovered but not collected before the original judgment or ruling took effect;
(3) Evidence collected but not cross-examined before the original judgment or ruling took effect;
(4) The expert opinion, inspection or examination record, or other evidence on which the original judgment or ruling was based has been altered or negated.

Article 380: For a case in which the People's Procuratorate files a protest in accordance with the trial supervision procedure, the people's court shall open a case within one month after receiving the protest letter. However, if any of the following circumstances exists, the case shall be handled differently:
(1) If the case is not within the jurisdiction of the court, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(2) If the protest letter cannot be served on the protested original defendant in accordance with the address provided in the protest letter, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to provide a new address of the original defendant within three days; if it fails to do so within the time limit, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(3) If the protest is based on new evidence but the relevant evidentiary materials are not attached, or the relevant evidence does not point to the facts originally prosecuted, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to supplement the relevant materials within three days; if it fails to do so within the time limit, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate.
For a protest case that has been decided to be returned, if the People's Procuratorate files a protest again after supplementing the relevant materials, and upon examination it meets the conditions for acceptance, the people's court shall accept it.

Article 389 After a retrial case has been retried, it shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the original judgment or ruling is correct in finding facts and applying law, and the sentence is appropriate, a ruling shall be made to dismiss the petition or protest and affirm the original judgment or ruling;
(2) If the original judgment or ruling is accurate in conviction and appropriate in sentence, but has defects in finding facts or applying law, a ruling shall be made to correct the defects and affirm the original judgment or ruling;
(3) If the original judgment or ruling has no error in finding facts but has error in applying law or the sentence is improper, the original judgment or ruling shall be quashed and a new judgment shall be rendered according to law;
(4) For a case tried under the procedure of second instance, if the facts in the original judgment or ruling are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, a new judgment may be rendered after the facts are clarified, or a ruling may be made to quash the original judgment and remand the case to the original People's Court for retrial.
If the facts in the original judgment or ruling are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, and after trial the facts have been clarified, the court shall render a judgment according to law based on the clarified facts; if the facts still cannot be clarified and the evidence is insufficient to find the defendant guilty, the original judgment or ruling shall be quashed and a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant.

Article 392 For the purposes of this Interpretation, foreign-related criminal cases refer to:
(1) Criminal cases in which a foreigner commits a crime within the territory of the People's Republic of China, or a Chinese citizen infringes upon the lawful rights and interests of a foreigner;
(2) Cases in which a Chinese citizen commits a crime outside the territory of the People's Republic of China that meets the circumstances specified in Article 7 and Article 10 of the Criminal Law;
(3) Cases in which a foreigner commits a crime against the State or a citizen of the People's Republic of China that meets the circumstances specified in Article 8 and Article 10 of the Criminal Law;
(4) Cases in which the People's Republic of China exercises jurisdiction within the scope of its obligations under international treaties it has undertaken, as specified in Article 9 of the Criminal Law.

Article 397 Where it is necessary to notify the embassy or consulate of a relevant country in China of relevant matters, the matter shall be reported level by level to the Higher People's Court, which shall give notice in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) If the country of nationality of the foreign party has concluded a bilateral consular treaty with China, the matter shall be handled in accordance with the provisions of the treaty; if no bilateral consular treaty has been concluded but the country is a party to the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, the matter shall be handled in accordance with the provisions of the Convention; if neither a consular treaty nor the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations applies but diplomatic relations exist with China, the matter may be handled in accordance with the opinions of the foreign affairs department, on the basis of reciprocity, and in accordance with relevant provisions and international practice;
(2) If a foreign-related criminal case occurs within the consular district of a foreign consulate in China, the relevant consulate of that country in the region shall be notified; if it occurs outside the consular district, the relevant embassy of that country in China shall be notified; for a country that has diplomatic relations with China but has no embassy or consulate in China, the embassy or consulate of its protecting power in China may be notified; if there is no protecting power or the protecting power is unknown, no notification is required;
(3) If the bilateral consular treaty prescribes a time limit for notification, notification shall be given within the prescribed time limit; if no such time limit is prescribed, notification shall be given as soon as possible in accordance with or by reference to the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations and international practice, and in no case later than seven days;
(4) If the bilateral consular treaty does not require notification and the foreign party requests that the embassy or consulate of his or her country of nationality in China not be notified, notification may be omitted, but the party shall provide a written statement to that effect.
When the Higher People's Court notifies the foreign embassy or consulate in China of relevant matters, it may, where necessary, seek the assistance of the foreign affairs department of the people's government.

Article 412 When serving criminal litigation documents on a party residing outside the territory of the People's Republic of China, the People's Court may adopt the following methods:
(1) Serving in accordance with the method prescribed by an international treaty concluded or jointly acceded to by the country where the recipient is located and the People's Republic of China;
(2) Serving through diplomatic channels;
(3) For a party of Chinese nationality, commissioning the embassy or consulate of China in the country where the recipient is located to serve on behalf;
(4) If the party is a private prosecutor in a case of private prosecution or a plaintiff in an incidental civil action, serving on the litigation agent who has the authority to accept service on his or her behalf;
(5) If the party is a foreign entity, serving on its representative office established within the territory of the People's Republic of China, or on its branch or business agent authorized to accept service;
(6) If the law of the country where the recipient is located permits, serving by mail; if the receipt of service is not returned within three months from the date of mailing, but it is sufficient to determine that service has been effected based on the circumstances, service shall be deemed to have been completed;
(7) If the law of the country where the recipient is located permits, serving by fax, email, or other means capable of confirming receipt by the recipient.

Article 418 If, after receiving the execution order for the death penalty but before execution, the People's Court of first instance discovers any of the following circumstances, it shall suspend the execution and immediately report the request for suspension of execution together with the relevant materials level by level to the Supreme People's Court:
(1) The criminal may have committed other crimes;
(2) Other criminal suspects in the joint crime have been brought to justice, which may affect the sentencing of the criminal;
(3) The execution of the death penalty on other criminals in the joint crime has been suspended or stopped, which may affect the sentencing of the criminal;
(4) The criminal has disclosed major criminal facts or has other major meritorious performances, which may require a retrial;
(5) The criminal is pregnant;
(6) The judgment or ruling may have other errors affecting the conviction or sentencing.
If the Supreme People's Court, upon examination, considers that the circumstances may affect the conviction or sentencing of the criminal, it shall rule to suspend the execution of the death penalty; if it considers that there is no such effect, it shall decide to continue the execution of the death penalty.

Article 422 The Supreme People's Court shall handle a case in which the execution of the death penalty has been suspended according to the following circumstances:
(1) If it is confirmed that the criminal is pregnant, the sentence shall be commuted;
(2) If it is confirmed that the criminal has other crimes that shall be prosecuted according to law, a ruling shall be made not to approve the death penalty, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(3) If it is confirmed that there is an error in the original judgment or ruling or that the criminal has a major meritorious performance requiring a retrial, a ruling shall be made not to approve the death penalty, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(4) If it is confirmed that there is no error in the original judgment or ruling, the criminal has no major meritorious performance, or the major meritorious performance does not affect the execution of the original judgment or ruling, a ruling shall be made to continue the execution of the death penalty, and the president shall reissue the execution order for the death penalty.

Article 428 After the execution of the death penalty, the People's Court responsible for the execution shall handle the following matters:
(1) It shall promptly review the criminal's testamentary letters and records of dying words; if such materials involve property succession, debt settlement, family instructions, or other matters, it shall deliver the testamentary letters and records of dying words to the family members, and at the same time make copies to be kept in the case file for reference; if such materials involve case leads or other issues, it shall send copies to the relevant authorities;
(2) It shall notify the criminal's family members to collect the criminal's ashes within a specified period; if there are no cremation conditions or if cremation is inappropriate due to ethnic, religious, or other reasons, it shall notify them to collect the corpse; if the corpse or ashes are not collected within the time limit, the People's Court shall notify the relevant units to handle the matter, and require such units to provide a description of the handling; the handling of the criminal's ashes or corpse shall be recorded in the case file;
(3) After executing the death penalty on a foreign criminal, the procedures and time limits for notifying the foreign embassy or consulate in China shall be handled in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 433 Where a criminal on temporary service of sentence outside prison falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court that originally made the decision on temporary service of sentence outside prison shall, within fifteen days after receiving the recommendation for execution in prison from the executing authority, make a decision to execute the sentence in prison:
(1) The conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison are not met;
(2) Leaving the city or county of residence without approval, and after a warning, refusing to correct, or refusing to report whereabouts and escaping from supervision;
(3) Receiving a public security penalty for violating supervision and administration regulations and still failing to correct;
(4) Receiving two warnings from the executing authority and still failing to correct;
(5) During the period of medical parole, failing to submit the disease review report as required, and after a warning, refusing to correct;
(6) After the circumstances of temporary service of sentence outside prison have ceased to exist, the sentence has not been completed;
(7) The guarantor loses the guarantor qualification or is disqualified for failing to perform obligations, and no new guarantor is provided within the prescribed time limit;
(8) Other circumstances in violation of laws, administrative regulations, and supervision and administration regulations that are serious in nature.
A decision on execution in prison made by a People's Court shall take effect immediately upon its issuance.

Article 443 In the process of executing a pecuniary penalty, where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall rule to suspend execution:
(1) The subject matter of execution is the object of a dispute in a case being tried by a People's Court or an arbitration institution, and ownership needs to be determined after the case is concluded;
(2) A person not involved in the case raises an objection to the subject matter of execution;
(3) Other circumstances under which execution shall be suspended.
After the cause for suspension is eliminated, execution shall be resumed.

Article 444 In the process of executing a pecuniary penalty, where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall rule to terminate execution:
(1) The judgment or ruling on the basis of which execution is carried out has been revoked;
(2) The person subject to enforcement has died or has been executed, and there is no property available for execution;
(3) The entity sentenced to a fine has been dissolved, and there is no property available for execution;
(4) The fine is exempted in accordance with Article 53 of the Criminal Law;
(5) Other circumstances under which execution shall be terminated.
After the ruling to terminate execution is made, if it is discovered that the property of the person subject to enforcement has been concealed or transferred, the property shall be recovered.

Article 449 Cases of commutation of punishment and parole shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) Commutation of punishment for a criminal sentenced to death with a two-year reprieve shall be ruled by the Higher People's Court of the place where the criminal serves the sentence, based on the recommendation for commutation that has been reviewed and approved by the prison administration authority at the same level;
(2) Commutation of punishment or parole for a criminal sentenced to life imprisonment shall be ruled by the Higher People's Court of the place where the criminal serves the sentence within one month after receiving the recommendation for commutation or parole that has been reviewed and approved by the prison administration authority at the same level; where the case is complex or the circumstances are special, the time limit may be extended by one month;
(3) Commutation of punishment or parole for a criminal sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment or whose sentence has been commuted to fixed-term imprisonment shall be ruled by the Intermediate People's Court of the place where the criminal serves the sentence within one month after receiving the recommendation for commutation or parole submitted by the executing authority; where the case is complex or the circumstances are special, the time limit may be extended by one month;
(4) Commutation of punishment for a criminal sentenced to criminal detention or public surveillance shall be ruled by the Intermediate People's Court of the place where the criminal serves the sentence within one month after receiving the recommendation for commutation or parole that has been reviewed and approved by the executing authority at the same level.
For commutation of punishment for a criminal on temporary service of sentence outside prison, the relevant provisions of the preceding paragraph shall be applied separately according to the circumstances.

Article 450 When accepting a case of commutation of punishment or parole, it shall be examined whether the materials transferred by the executing authority include the following:
(1) The recommendation for commutation of punishment or parole;
(2) Copies of the adjudicative documents of the final instance court, the execution notice, and all previous rulings on commutation of punishment;
(3) Written materials proving the specific facts of the criminal's genuine repentance, meritorious service, or major meritorious service;
(4) The criminal's evaluation and assessment form, reward and punishment approval form, etc.;
(5) An investigation and assessment report on the impact of the criminal's parole on the community where he or she will reside;
(6) Other materials that need to be transferred based on the circumstances of the case.
After examination, if the materials are incomplete, the executing authority that submitted the recommendation for commutation or parole shall be notified to supplement the materials.

Article 452 When trying a case of commutation of punishment or parole, the following information shall be publicly announced:
(1) The criminal's basic personal information such as name and age;
(2) The crime and sentence determined in the original judgment;
(3) The criminal's history of previous commutations of punishment;
(4) The executing authority's recommendation for commutation of punishment or parole and the basis thereof.
The public announcement shall specify the announcement period and the method for submitting opinions. The announcement shall be posted in the public areas of the criminal's place of confinement; where conditions permit, it may be made public to society.

Article 453 When trying a case of commutation of punishment or parole, a collegial panel shall be formed, and the case may be tried on the basis of written materials, but the following cases shall be tried in open court:
(1) Where commutation of punishment is requested due to the criminal's major meritorious service;
(2) Where the starting time, interval, or extent of the requested commutation does not comply with the general provisions;
(3) Where the case has significant social impact or high public attention;
(4) Where a complaint opinion is received during the public announcement period;
(5) Where the People's Procuratorate raises an objection;
(6) Other cases where a trial in open court is necessary.

Article 458 Where a criminal on probation or parole, during the probation or parole period, falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court that originally made the judgment or ruling on probation or parole shall, within one month after receiving the recommendation for revocation of probation or parole from the executing authority, make a ruling to revoke the probation or parole:
(1) Violating the restraining order under serious circumstances;
(2) Without justifiable reason, failing to report on time as required or being away from supervision during community correction for more than one month;
(3) Receiving a public security penalty for violating supervision and administration regulations and still failing to correct;
(4) Receiving three warnings from the executing authority and still failing to correct;
(5) Other circumstances in violation of relevant laws, administrative regulations, and supervision and administration regulations that are serious in nature.
A ruling by the People's Court to revoke probation or parole shall take effect immediately upon its issuance.
The People's Court shall deliver the ruling on revocation of probation or parole to the judicial administrative organ at the county level where the criminal resides, which shall hand over the criminal for execution in accordance with relevant provisions. The ruling on revocation of probation or parole shall also be served simultaneously to the People's Procuratorate and the public security organ at the same level where the criminal resides.

Article 507: Where, according to the provisions of the Criminal Law, illegal gains and other property involved in the case shall be recovered, and any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Procuratorate may submit an application for confiscation of illegal gains to the People's Court:
(1) The criminal suspect or defendant has committed serious crimes such as embezzlement, bribery, or terrorist activities, and then fled, and after being wanted for one year, cannot be brought to justice;
(2) The criminal suspect or defendant has died.

Article 508: Any of the following circumstances shall be considered a "serious criminal case" as specified in Article 280, paragraph 1, of the Criminal Procedure Law:
(1) The criminal suspect or defendant may be sentenced to a punishment of life imprisonment or above;
(2) The case has a significant impact within the province, autonomous region, municipality directly under the Central Government, or the whole country;
(3) Other serious criminal cases.

Article 510: With respect to an application for confiscation of illegal gains submitted by the People's Procuratorate, the People's Court shall examine the following:
(1) Whether it falls within the jurisdiction of this court;
(2) Whether it specifies the circumstances of the criminal suspect or defendant's alleged involvement in the relevant crime and attaches relevant evidence materials;
(3) Whether a wanted notice or death certificate is attached;
(4) Whether it lists the types, quantities, and locations of the illegal gains and other property involved in the case, and attaches relevant evidence materials;
(5) Whether it attaches a list of the sealed up, seized, or frozen illegal gains and other property involved in the case and the relevant legal formalities;
(6) Whether it specifies the names, addresses, contact information, and demands of the close relatives of the criminal suspect or defendant and other interested parties;
(7) Whether it specifies the reasons and legal basis for the application for confiscation.

Article 511: The People's Court shall complete the review of an application for confiscation of illegal gains within seven days, and shall handle it according to the following circumstances:
(1) If it does not fall within the jurisdiction of this court, it shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(2) If the materials are incomplete, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to supplement them within three days;
(3) If it falls within the scope of acceptance of the confiscation of illegal gains procedure and the jurisdiction of this court, and the materials are complete, it shall be accepted.
Where the People's Procuratorate has not yet sealed up, seized, or frozen the property for which confiscation is applied, or the period for sealing up, seizing, or freezing is about to expire, and there is a risk that the property involved in the case may be concealed, transferred, damaged, or destroyed, the People's Court may seal up, seize, or freeze the property for which confiscation is applied.

Article 512: After deciding to accept the application for confiscation of illegal gains, the People's Court shall issue a public notice within fifteen days, with a notice period of six months. The public notice shall specify the following:
(1) The cause of action;
(2) The basic information about the criminal suspect or defendant being wanted while at large or deceased;
(3) The types, quantities, and locations of the property for which confiscation is applied;
(4) The time limit and method for the close relatives of the criminal suspect or defendant and other interested parties to apply to participate in the proceedings;
(5) Other circumstances that shall be announced.
The public notice shall be published in a newspaper with national circulation or on the official website of the People's Court, and shall be posted and published on the court's bulletin board; where necessary, it may be posted and published at the place where the crime was committed, the place of residence of the criminal suspect or defendant, and the location of the immovable property for which confiscation is applied.
Where the People's Court already has the contact information of the close relatives of the criminal suspect or defendant and other interested parties, it shall directly inform them of the content of the public notice by telephone, fax, email, or other means, and shall record this in the case file.

Article 515: When hearing a case concerning an application for confiscation of illegal gains in court, the following procedures shall be followed:
(1) After the presiding judge announces the beginning of the court investigation, the prosecutor shall first read the application, followed by the interested parties and litigation agents expressing their opinions;
(2) The court shall investigate in turn whether the criminal suspect or defendant has committed serious crimes such as embezzlement, bribery, or terrorist activities and has been wanted for one year without being brought to justice, or whether he or she has died, and whether the property for which confiscation is applied shall be recovered according to law; during the investigation, the prosecutor shall first present the relevant evidence, followed by the interested parties expressing opinions, presenting relevant evidence, and conducting cross-examination;
(3) During the court debate stage, the prosecutor shall first speak, followed by the interested parties and their litigation agents speaking and engaging in debate.
Where an interested party, after being notified, refuses to appear in court without justifiable reasons, or withdraws from the court without the court's permission during the proceedings, the court may convert the hearing to a non-court hearing, unless there are other interested parties participating in the proceedings.

Article 516: In a case of application for confiscation of illegal gains, after trial, the People's Court shall handle according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts are clear, evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the property subject to confiscation is indeed illegal gains or other property involved in the case, except for returning to the victim according to law, it shall rule to confiscate;
(2) If it does not meet the conditions set forth in Article 507 of this Interpretation, it shall rule to reject the application.

Article 518: With respect to appeals or protests against the ruling of the first instance on confiscation of illegal gains or dismissal of the application, the People's Court of second instance, after trial, shall make a ruling according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the original ruling is correct, it shall dismiss the appeal or protest and affirm the original ruling;
(2) If the original ruling is indeed erroneous, it may modify the original ruling after ascertaining the facts, or may revoke the original ruling and remand for retrial;
(3) If the original trial violated legal procedures and may affect fair adjudication, it shall revoke the original ruling and remand for retrial.

Article 526: For an application for compulsory medical treatment filed by the People's Procuratorate, the People's Court shall examine the following:
(1) whether it falls within the jurisdiction of this court;
(2) whether it states the identity of the respondent, the time, place, means, and damage caused by the violent act, and attaches relevant evidence;
(3) whether it attaches a forensic psychiatric evaluation opinion and other evidence proving that the respondent is a mentally ill person who does not bear criminal liability according to law;
(4) whether it lists the name, address, and contact information of the respondent's legal representative;
(5) other matters requiring examination.

Article 527: For an application for compulsory medical treatment filed by the People's Procuratorate, the People's Court shall complete the examination within seven days and handle according to the following circumstances:
(1) if it is not within the jurisdiction of this court, it shall return the application to the People's Procuratorate;
(2) if the materials are incomplete, it shall notify the People's Procuratorate to supplement them within three days;
(3) if it falls within the scope of acceptance of the compulsory medical treatment procedure and the jurisdiction of this court, and the materials are complete, it shall accept the application.

Article 530: When holding a trial in open court for a case applying for compulsory medical treatment, the following procedures shall be followed:
(1) After the presiding judge announces the start of court investigation, the prosecutor shall first read the application, followed by the respondent's legal representative and litigation agent expressing opinions;
(2) The court shall investigate in turn whether the respondent committed violent acts endangering public security or seriously endangering personal safety of citizens, whether the respondent is a mentally ill person who does not bear criminal liability according to law, and whether the respondent is likely to continue to endanger society; during the investigation, the prosecutor shall first present relevant evidence, followed by the respondent's legal representative and litigation agent expressing opinions and presenting relevant evidence, and cross-examination;
(3) During the court debate stage, the prosecutor shall first speak, followed by the respondent's legal representative and litigation agent speaking, and then debate.
If the respondent requests to appear in court, the People's Court shall, after examining the respondent's physical and mental condition and deeming it appropriate, permit the appearance. The respondent appearing in court may express opinions during the court investigation and debate stages.
After the prosecutor reads the application, if the respondent's legal representative and litigation agent have no objections, the court investigation may be simplified.

Article 531: After trial of a case applying for compulsory medical treatment, the People's Court shall handle according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the conditions for compulsory medical treatment as provided in Article 284 of the Criminal Procedure Law are met, it shall make a decision to impose compulsory medical treatment on the respondent;
(2) If the respondent is a mentally ill person who does not bear criminal liability according to law but does not meet the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, it shall make a decision to dismiss the application for compulsory medical treatment; if the respondent has caused harmful results, it shall at the same time order the respondent's family members or guardians to keep strict custody and provide medical treatment;
(3) If the respondent has full or partial criminal capacity and shall be investigated for criminal liability according to law, it shall make a decision to dismiss the application for compulsory medical treatment and return the case to the People's Procuratorate for handling according to law.

Article 533: After trial of a case as provided in the preceding article, the People's Court shall handle according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the defendant meets the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, it shall pronounce a judgment that the defendant does not bear criminal liability and at the same time make a decision to impose compulsory medical treatment on the defendant;
(2) If the defendant is a mentally ill person who does not bear criminal liability according to law but does not meet the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, it shall pronounce a judgment that the defendant is not guilty or does not bear criminal liability; if the defendant has caused harmful results, it shall at the same time order the defendant's family members or guardians to keep strict custody and provide medical treatment;
(3) If the defendant has full or partial criminal capacity and shall be investigated for criminal liability according to law, it shall continue trial in accordance with ordinary procedure.

Article 537: For an application for reconsideration against a compulsory medical treatment decision, the People's Court at the next higher level shall form a collegial panel to try the case and, within one month, make a reconsideration decision according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the person subject to compulsory medical treatment meets the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, it shall dismiss the reconsideration application and affirm the original decision;
(2) If the person subject to compulsory medical treatment does not meet the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, it shall revoke the original decision;
(3) If the original trial violated legal procedures and may affect fair adjudication, it shall revoke the original decision and remand the case to the original trial People's Court for retrial.
